// src/compiler/parser/ast/normalize/mod.tg
// Span normalization for the surface AST (ADR 29.6.26f — Decisions, §2.2, AC 7).
//
// The structural comparator compares ASTs EXACTLY, spans included. That is a
// deliberate policy choice, not an oversight: silently stripping spans inside
// `assert_eq` would let a test pass on a node built from the wrong source
// range, which is precisely the class of bug parser tests exist to catch.
//
// A test that genuinely wants to ignore source positions therefore normalizes
// EXPLICITLY, by calling one of the functions below before asserting:
//
//     assert_eq(strip_spans_expr(parsed), strip_spans_expr(expected))
//
// Every function here rewrites each `Span` it reaches to `canonical_span()` and
// is otherwise structure-preserving, so two ASTs differing only in spans become
// structurally identical. Coverage is the whole `Expr`-reachable graph — the
// mutually recursive `Expr` / `TypeExpr` / `Pattern` / `Stmt` / `Item` cluster
// plus every span-carrying record between them. A partial normalizer would be
// worse than none: it would pass through un-normalized nodes and report a
// mismatch the caller has no way to interpret.
//
// Types that carry no `Span` anywhere (`Visibility`, `BinOp`, `UnaryOp`,
// `PatLit`, `MaybeString`, and the scalar leaves) are passed through untouched
// rather than given identity functions.
//
// ## THE DRIVER NEVER REACHES THIS SUBSYSTEM (ADR 3.9.26a)
//
// Nothing on a driver path imports these modules. `main.tg` declares
// `mod parser;`, so they are compiled and type-checked on every build and read
// as compiler machinery, but the only importers are the AST-comparison entry
// files `test_ast_compare.tg`, `test_ast_compare_cluster.tg` and
// `mustfail_ast_compare.tg`.
//
// Unlike `src/compiler/codegen/`, which is test-only because `run_compile`
// delegates codegen to the bootstrap binary and will stop being so when the
// self-host emits its own machine code, this subsystem is test-only BY DESIGN
// and permanently: the paragraphs above are the argument for why the compiler
// itself must never normalize spans. A driver path importing anything here
// would be the defect, not the fix.
//
// Census: `tungsten doctor audit-driver-reach src/compiler/main.tg`.

pub mod common;
pub mod types;
pub mod patterns;
pub mod exprs;
pub mod nodes;
pub mod items;

// Re-export so callers can `use parser::ast::normalize::{strip_spans_expr, ...}`
// without knowing which file a given node type lives in.
pub use parser::ast::normalize::common::*;
pub use parser::ast::normalize::types::*;
pub use parser::ast::normalize::patterns::*;
pub use parser::ast::normalize::exprs::*;
pub use parser::ast::normalize::nodes::*;
pub use parser::ast::normalize::items::*;
